Multiply longs by constants without a register for the high word.

The compiler loaded both words of a constant multiplier into registers and
multiplied with UMULL and two MLA. A constant whose high word is 0 or -1,
which covers every constant that fits in 32 bits, needs one register and
one MLA: for -1 the missing term is a subtraction of the low word of the
other operand. Multiplication by a power of two is a shift, and by -1 a
negation. Besides saving the instructions, the missing register eases the
register pressure in loops that multiply by a constant.

And, or and exclusive or with an immediate word that leaves the word
unchanged no longer emit an instruction when the result already lives in
the register of the operand.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/cpu/arm/CodeGenerator_arm.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/cpu/arm/CodeGenerator_arm.cpp
@@ -2705,6 +2705,12 @@
 }
 #endif  // ENABLE_FLOAT
 
+// Whether a logical operation with this immediate leaves the word unchanged.
+static inline bool is_logical_identity(Assembler::Opcode opcode, jint imm) {
+  return (opcode == Assembler::_andr && imm == -1) ||
+         ((opcode == Assembler::_orr || opcode == Assembler::_eor) && imm == 0);
+}
